before:
```typescript
const scores: number[] = [3, 6];
scores.push(9);
console.log(scores.join(","));
```

This prints 3,6,9. scores.push(9) receives one number, appends it to the same array, and returns the new length (unused here). scores.join(",") receives a separator string and returns a new text representation without changing scores. The dot selects the array method and parentheses pass its argument.

after:
Push adds an item to the same array. Join makes text from its items using the separator you give it.
```typescript
const scores: number[] = [3, 6];
scores.push(9);
console.log(scores.join(","));
```
The output is 3,6,9.
